**Chapter 10-4: "You Are Welcome"**

"―We urgently need to develop something like a steam engine, or rather, a magic stone engine."

Holding the reins on the driver's seat and gazing straight ahead, Natsuki Subaru said that. At Subaru's words, Beatrice, who was perched neatly on his lap, furrowed her brow.

"...You've started saying strange things again, I suppose. What is this 'Majiki Kikan' thing?"

"It's technology that doesn't exist in this world yet. Like, if we could create a system similar to a steam engine using magic stone energy, maybe we could make something like a locomotive work."

"Kikansha?"

"Yeah, an iron dragon carriage that doesn't need a ground dragon and can run indefinitely as long as the magic stones don't run out. It can only run where there are rails, so it's low on freedom, but in exchange, you can travel back and forth at a pretty good pace within the connected rail network. How's that?"

"I suppose it sounds convenient, I suppose. If one ignores how fantastical it is, in fact."

At Beatrice's answer, which didn't hide her exasperation, Subaru pursed his lips to express his dissatisfaction.

He thought it was a fairly decent idea, but as expected, without knowing the actual thing, it was hard to get the image across. Of course, in terms of not knowing about actual locomotives, Subaru and Beatrice were about even. If only he knew a little more about how steam engines worked, he could have a more realistic conversation.

"――――ッ"

"Whoa, sorry, sorry, Patrasche. I'm not trying to disregard you, who came all the way to the Empire to pick me up. This is purely a matter of the right tool for the job, so to speak..."

"――――ッ!"

"Unfortunately, it seems you can't smooth things over with that, I suppose. If you upset Patrasche's mood, it will take even longer to get back, in fact."

"That's a situation I absolutely want to avoid... Please, cheer up―"

Even if they couldn't strictly share the exact image of a locomotive, it seemed Patrasche had understood that its role would overlap with her own. Scratching his head at the protest from his beloved dragon, Subaru gripped the reins again and narrowed his eyes at the unchanging sandy scenery.

――After sealing Al at the Pleiades Watchtower, Subaru and the others, having learned the facts revealed in the loop that was erased and the circumstances they needed to know, set out for the royal capital.

The shock of Al's betrayal was great, and to swallow that, the group left the tower with high spirits, even if forced ― however, the road to the royal capital was long.

After all, it was the distance between the far eastern and far western ends of the Lugunica Kingdom, essentially crossing the entire kingdom.

Even with the help of the energetic and excellent Patrasche's running power, it was expected to take a considerable number of days, and the frustration only grew with their initial momentum.

Given such a trying state of mind, it was almost inevitable that Subaru's head would wander to the search for new means of transportation as a form of escape.

"Actually, if locomotives or trains could be realized, I wonder how much travel time would shrink... There's no doubt they could move faster than ground dragons without resting."

He thought that maybe, when things were a bit more settled and they had some breathing room, he should more seriously discuss it with Roswaal or Otto.

The chain dragon carriages devised in the Empire were close in concept, but since they still relied on the running power of ground dragons and other riding beasts, a magic stone engine should be superior in convenience.

"If we could make it, I feel like we could blow Abel's mind. Just imagining him looking all frustrated and asking how we did it makes it feel worth doing."

"If it involves magic stones, Betty might be able to consult, but if it's for a silly purpose like wanting to one-up that Abel, I won't cooperate, I suppose."

"Right. Abel's frustrated face is a silly reason. Let's forget it, forget it."

Waving his hand to disperse the hazy image of Abel's scowling face that had floated up, Subaru rested his chin on Beatrice's head and let out a small sigh. Naturally, Beatrice seemed to become aware of the sigh above her head and the hard sensation against the back of her head ― the black sphere hanging from Subaru's neck.

"Subaru, that forbidden spell is―"

"It's something you deciphered for me. ...Sorry that's the only explanation I can give."

"I'm not blaming you, I suppose. What Betty is concerned about is the abnormal precision of that magic's structure and its target selection method... It reminded me of a fragment of Mother, in fact."

"Mother... Echidna, huh?"
"I suppose."

With a small nod from Beatrice, Subaru recalled the 'Witch of Greed' he encountered in the 'Sanctuary'.

Just in terms of that infuriating face, he had also seen it during the meeting with Sphinx, who had taken her form amidst the 'Great Calamity' in the Vollachia Empire. Considering the depth and multitude of the scars left by that 'Witch of Greed', Subaru had lost faith in the credibility of the exchange in the Tomb.

Moreover, the existence of the 'Witch of Greed' had been erased too meticulously compared to the scars she left all over this world.

It wasn't something that had faded with the passage of time; it felt more natural to think it had been intentionally erased from history. ―The problem was, who did it?

"Technically, since Echidna was dead, it wasn't done by her. Well, given the story that she was still entering people's dreams and causing trouble after death, that becomes suspicious too."

"That shouldn't be possible, I suppose. Freely traversing people's dreams even after death is something even a dream mage, a practitioner of dream magic, shouldn't be able to do, in fact."

"Huh? Dream mage? Dream magic?"

"It's magic that interferes with a person's mind using dreams as a medium, I suppose. It's also a type of forbidden art, and its practitioners have been almost completely eradicated, in fact."

"There's definitely survivors of that! Interfering with minds? Sounds like they just implanted convenient memories of 'we eradicated them!' and let the real ones get away!"

"Y-you're being too pessimistic, I suppose! ...Ahem, at any rate, I suppose. The approach Mother used that you and Emilia encountered in the Tomb seems to apply the principles of dream magic, but strictly speaking, it's different, so she shouldn't be able to traverse the consciousness of the living as if crossing through dreams, in fact."

Subaru nodded at Beatrice, who waved a raised finger while giving a cute lecture.

He didn't understand the detailed principles, but it seemed that Echidna's dream castle was ultimately centered on the special place that was the Tomb and didn't extend outside ― or so it appeared.

If that were the case, then the concern that 'Echidna did it all by herself' would vanish.

"But in that case, it means someone inheriting Echidna's will has been diligently working on the cover-up, and the one with the highest probability for that is Roswaal..."

"If the perpetrator is from within our circle, we'll just have to grit our teeth and expose them, I suppose."

"That's why I can't expose him until the Royal Selection is over, and even after it's over, I'd rather not if possible. Also, the plot twist where Beatrice, who's acting like a pretty girl in front of me, is actually secretly active as Echidna's apostle behind the scenes is a staple of entertainment, right?"

"Betty as the final boss? Such a plot was scrapped long ago, I suppose. It lacks even a shred of persuasiveness or consistency; it's just a bad idea wanting to surprise people, in fact."

"I think so too, but you sure lay it on thick..."

As if unwilling to even hear it in jest that she would become Subaru's enemy. That too was proof of how much she cherished Subaru, so he allowed himself a warm feeling for now.

With all that, assuming that the one who went around erasing Echidna's traces was neither Roswaal nor, of course, Beatrice ― then,

"――The Witch Cult."

"――――"

Beatrice shifted slightly and held her breath. That tense reaction from Beatrice was proof that she inwardly agreed with Subaru's muttering.

The 'Witch of Greed' ― no, not just information about Echidna, but any of the 'Witches' bearing the names of the deadly sins other than the 'Witch of Envy', their very existence was being erased from history.

And the Archbishops of the Deadly Sins who appeared simultaneously in the watergate city of Priestella each made their own selfish demands on the city in exchange for the lives of its residents. At that time, there were some like Regulus who simply tried to satisfy their own desires, but mixed in were also demands that were hard to believe the individuals themselves would want.

"Also, I heard from Otto before. No matter where in the world you are, if you accidentally find something related to a 'Witch', the Witch Cult will come to take it... something like that."

In fact, the fortress city of Garkla in Vollachia that Subaru and the others visited was said to have been devastated as a result of that Regulus running amok. He kept remembering people he didn't want to remember, putting him in a bad mood.

Anyway ―,

"The rampage in Priestella feels like it confirms the rumor Otto talked about. If that's the case, their rampages aren't just terrorism by a cult; there's another motive ― perhaps precisely to hide the 'Witches' from this world."

The Witch Cult reveres the 'Witch of Envy' and considers worshiping her existence to be good.

Because of this surface-level information being accepted as common sense, the fact that they are trying to erase the traces of 'Witches' other than the 'Witch of Envy' might be hidden.

"Hiding a tree in the forest, or rather, hiding 'Witches' within the episodes of the 'Witch of Envy', huh... But if that's the case, then the ones who created the Witch Cult are―"

"――It cannot be Mother, I suppose."

Following the same train of thought as Subaru, Beatrice preemptively denied that conclusion.

Her lips pursed tightly, and the expression on Beatrice's face as she looked forward was invisible to Subaru. But the emotion carried in those words was not so much conviction as a fragile, feeble prayer.

Logically, she probably understood that Subaru's reasoning made sense.

However, the fact that Echidna was her creator and a beloved family member with whom she spent precious time prevented Beatrice from ever accepting it.

That was painfully clear to Subaru, and at the same time, it fueled his anger towards Echidna.

"Making my cute Beako feel this miserable..."

The 'Witch' who ordered Beatrice to 'wait for that person' and left her in the forbidden library for four hundred years.

Subaru knew that the true intent behind Echidna's instruction was a terrifyingly wicked curiosity to see who Beatrice would choose as 'that person'. Of course, he couldn't tell Beatrice that directly. Nor did he intend to.

It was a vulgar and shameful way of thinking to try to negate Beatrice's feelings for Echidna and overwrite them with his own. He simply needed to become an even more significant presence to Beatrice while she continued to cherish Echidna.

"Beako, I love you."

"――!? The flow of conversation changed, I suppose!?"

Overflowing with affection and a sense of mission, Subaru hugged Beatrice tightly from behind. At that embrace, Beatrice's pensive face turned bright red. This was it, this.

With this, not to dismiss this discussion as inconsequential nonsense, but―

"We can't find the answer here yet. We'll go to the royal capital... and huh, we end up back there again. Damn it, the development of a magic stone locomotive is eagerly awaited...!"

Gripping the black sphere hanging against his chest, Subaru uttered this with impatience.

The road was still long, the royal capital they aimed for was far in the distance ― and,

However, Subaru's impatience was to be unexpectedly relieved.

That is ―,

"――My, my, I've grown tired of waiting. So you've finally returned."

It began with the surprise of being welcomed in the post town of Milula, which they reached after crossing the sandy sea, by a bald-headed giant with arms as thick as logs who was waiting for them ― Old Man Rom.




---



"――I am surprised there are still survivors among the Giant race, I suppose. Like the Oni race, I thought their bloodlines were almost extinct, in fact."

"Unfortunately, I haven't encountered any Giants other than myself. Depending on the circumstances, I might be the last surviving Giant in this world."

"Seriously? That's beyond countdown to extinction..."

Beatrice gazed intently at the colossal body before her, and Old Man Rom, the Giant in question, responded. Hearing that, Subaru furrowed his brows at the desperate situation of the Giant race.

After all, if the last survivor was only the extremely elderly Old Man Rom, there would be no hope for descendants, leaving only extinction. If the last survivor of the race were young, there might still have been a thread of possibility, but...

"Not at all. A race that has fulfilled its purpose dying out is a matter of course, I suppose. There's no reason to keep races like the Giants or Oni alive in the world to come. Live as you please, perish as you please... That's about it, I'd say."

"I'm not really fond of that kind of... fatalism? I mean, saying it after it's too late is pointless, though."

"Pious words, you're saying. You seem to have gained a bit of thoughtfulness yourself, lad."

Subaru expressed his frustration by prodding the inside of his cheek with his tongue, and Old Man Rom smiled with a twisted cheek, slapping Subaru's shoulder with his large palm.

Indeed, this was the first proper conversation Subaru had had with Old Man Rom since that day when the Royal Selection was declared, when they had sparred during the various attempts to infiltrate the castle.

He had heard Rom served as a brains trust for Felt, but meeting and speaking with him again after so long finally made that fact feel real.

"Looking back, Old Man Rom and Felt were people I met in this world even before Beako, so it's deeply moving... Thinking too deeply, it means I met the fruit shop uncle before Emilia-tan, which is kinda weird."

Fundamentally, if he started considering the order of meetings, things would get nonsensical and his head would bug.

In any case, reuniting with old acquaintances was a joyous thing for Subaru. All the more so if they were people he met on his very first day in this other world.

So――,

"――You're one of them too, Reinhardt."

"Thank goodness. I was worried you'd forgotten me, seeing you so engrossed in conversation with Rom-dono."

"Forgotten? You? Ever had that experience?"

That seemed impossible, but even if the Authority of Gluttony ate his 'name', Reinhardt's presence was such that he'd be impossible to forget. Or perhaps with him, a situation where his 'name' could be eaten wouldn't even occur.

At Subaru's pointed remark, Reinhardt shrugged with a wry smile.

"Fortunately, no such opportunity has arisen so far. Not that it doesn't feel like a burden at times. Because my own actions can't be undone."

As he said this, Reinhardt glanced briefly behind Subaru ― where Garfiel stood imposingly.

Exchanging only the briefest of greetings upon reuniting, Garfiel had been silently fixing Reinhardt with sharp emerald eyes. Bathed in that gaze, Reinhardt also slightly narrowed his own blue eyes.

"You've improved your skills considerably since last time, it seems. In such a short period, that's amazing."

"Tch, you say that so lightly. I've had to go through near-death experiences over and over just to level up, and yet your depths are more unfathomable than ever."

"Will you not challenge me like you did in Priestella anymore?"

"Right now, there's no path to victory. And even if I went and lost on my own, that ranking would just cause trouble for Emilia-sama."

Garfiel clicked his tongue and turned his face away, forcefully suppressing his fighting spirit.

It couldn't be called overthinking. In reality, since he was far from matching a knight's strength, it was Garfiel, Emilia camp's military officer, who would be compared to Reinhardt. If it became known that Garfiel had lost in a direct confrontation with Reinhardt, even if it was an obvious ranking to everyone, it risked leaving an impression of the camp's insufficient power. And Garfiel had thought that far and restrained himself.

"Garfiel, you... you've become an adult...!"

"Hah!? What's with that, Boss! Don't pat my head! I just said I can't win right now, that's pathetic! There's no reason to praise me!"

"There is, there is, there is, there is, there absolutely is! Hey, everyone, let's praise Garfiel!"

"G-grrr!"

Surrounding the shy, blushing Garfiel ― starting with Subaru, followed by Beatrice, Petra, and Meili ― they praised him with "good job, good job."

It was precisely the opposite pattern of when Subaru had been comforted at the Pleiades Watchtower.

After thoroughly doting on Garfiel in that manner――,

"――So then, what business do the two of you from Lady Felt's camp have with us?"

Indeed, the one who properly broached the main topic was Petra, the most responsible one in the group.

Placing both hands on her hips and acting the part of the camp's negotiator with gusto, Subaru felt an unexpected sense of reliability in Petra's small back, and his chest grew warm.

Unintentionally, he felt he could catch a glimpse of how desperately she must have fought to bring Subaru back during the battle with Al, a fragment of the lost loop.

"Wh-why are you making such a moved face, Subaru?"

"Nah, I might just be getting a bit weepy lately. Beako, can you hug me?"

"W-well, fine, I suppose. Such a clingy child, in fact."

Though looking reluctant, Beatrice switched from holding hands to clinging tightly to Subaru's waist. With that, Subaru focused on the conversation before him.

The reason Reinhardt and the others had gone out of their way to wait for them here――,

"Of course, to meet you. ―An urgent situation has arisen in the royal capital. Lady Emilia must be terribly anxious, or so Lady Felt judged and ordered."

"Emilia-sis? But then, why would Lady Felt..."

"They've become friends. So, she said she wants to lend a hand for her friend."

"My, my, looks like Felt-chan got charmed by Emilia-oneesama. I get that feeling. Maybe I could get along with Felt-chan too."

Reinhardt answered the doubtful Petra, and hearing that, Meili smiled mischievously. However, phrasing it that way meant admitting she herself had been won over by Emilia, but did Meili have that self-awareness? Subaru said nothing.

Besides, Subaru was also surprised. Of course, he never had the impression that Emilia and Felt's relationship was bad, but Felt taking action for Emilia...

"Hard to imagine from the first time, when the insignia got stolen..."

"I'll tell you now, lad, I'd rather you not spread that story far and wide. If you folks start spreading such bad rumors, I'll have to pull some tricks of my own."

"Since it's a case of reaping what you sowed... Well, digging up that incident would cause trouble for not just you, so I won't."

Subaru shrugged off Old Man Rom's preemptive jab.

That insignia theft incident in the royal capital was connected to Roswaal's schemes, as he was the one who hired Elsa from behind the scenes. And Elsa also involves Meili, who is right there, so if things get scrutinized, the ones with stomachaches would rather be the Emilia camp.

First of all, he doubted Emilia would bring up the theft by Felt now. All the more if they'd become friends.

"If anything, there's even a suspicion Emilia-tan might have forgotten Felt stole the insignia... Surely not?"

"I believe Lady Emilia, having overcome that past, still desired friendship with Lady Felt. That's what I think."

"Right. I prefer that Emilia-tan too. But..."

The problem was the fact that said Emilia was being driven into a state of terrible anxiety.

Wondering what on earth happened in the royal capital, Subaru suddenly realized the possibility that the cause of Emilia's current state of mind might be himself.

It might be the effect of the 'news' he asked Flam to deliver before leaving the Pleiades Watchtower――,

"No, I've also heard about Lady Priscilla and the matter with Al-dono. Lady Emilia's grief was immense, but she was rather worried about you all. So, the direct cause isn't that."

"Really? That's good... well, not good, but I understand. But if it's not that either, then what on earth happened?"

"――A situation that shakes the very foundations of the Royal Selection, you could call it, I suppose."

"「――!」"

At Old Man Rom's grave tone, the expressions of everyone in Subaru's group changed.

A situation shaking the foundations of the Royal Selection ― hearing that alone signaled a major crisis. The fact that Reinhardt didn't deny it was also proof that there was no doubt about that assessment.

Whether it was due to the deceased Priscilla dropping out as a Royal Selection candidate, or if there was another cause, was unclear, but――,

"Regarding that problem, it has become necessary for each camp to hold discussions. So, I, who had undertaken to fetch Rom-dono from his territory, came to meet you all directly."

"Then, you'd want to reunite with Felt in the capital as soon as possible too..."

"Latching and the others are in the capital. Following Lady Felt's command had higher priority in this case. I actually wanted to meet you at the tower, but heading into the Augria Sand Dunes without Meili's help risked missing you on the way."

"So, we waited for you folks in this town, you see."

Hearing Old Man Rom conclude, Subaru's questions finally settled in his mind.

With such circumstances, Reinhardt and Old Man Rom had waited for Subaru's group to arrive in Milula. While feeling grateful for that, it also became terrifying, wondering what problem could make them go that far.

However――,

"Can't just not hear about it. Time's precious. Let's talk while moving."

"That's right, let's do that. I also have an exceptionally large number of things I want to hear from you, Subaru. About what happened in the Empire after we parted in Priestella, within the scope of what you can say."

"There's nothing I can't tell you. I'll tell you everything. I also have several things I want to ask someone carrying a signboard as big as the 'Sword Saint'."

"――. In that case, anything I can answer, I will."

Nodding in agreement, a consensus was reached between Subaru's group and Reinhardt's.

For Subaru, who had felt frustrated enough with travel to yearn for a non-existent locomotive, being able to borrow Reinhardt's power was not just a timely aid, but like getting the Millennium Falcon.

And so, before setting out again for the royal capital――,

"Oh, right, Reinhardt."

"Hmm? What is it?"

"You probably won't understand, but hear me out. ―Thanks."

At Subaru's words of thanks, Reinhardt, as forewarned, raised an eyebrow, not understanding. Smiling slightly at the fact he had managed to surprise the 'Sword Saint', Subaru continued,

"I don't know the details either, but I just have this conviction that I must have caused you a hell of a lot of trouble. So, thank you."

"I can't recall anything that fits, so I'm not sure how to respond."

"Then just say 'You're welcome' or something."

"Understood. ―You're welcome."

"Your pronunciation is really good."

Feeling renewed gratitude towards Reinhardt, who responded that way even to unexpected thanks, Subaru patted the head of Beatrice clinging to his waist and began preparing to move.

Since Reinhardt had covered a distance that would normally take many days in one go, they were probably in for an incredible method of travel. He had to mentally prepare.

"――――"

Unaware that Reinhardt's eyes, his hand on the hilt of the sword at his waist, were watching Subaru's back as he made departure preparations with his comrades, as if sizing him up.
